+Reading from a file
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+You can show progress while a file is read with :func:`~rich.progress.open`. Pass a path (a ``str``, a path-like object, or ``bytes``). The default mode is text (``'r'``, which is the same as ``'rt'``), and the reader yields ``str``. Use ``'rb'`` to read bytes instead. The default total is the size of the file in bytes, so the bar fills as the file is consumed. The display stops when the ``with`` block exits, and the file is closed with it::
+
+    from rich.progress import open
+
+    with open("data.csv") as csv_file:
+        for line in csv_file:
+            process(line)
+
+    with open("image.png", "rb") as binary_file:
+        data = binary_file.read()
+
+Reading from a file-like object
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+If you already have an open binary file object, use :func:`~rich.progress.wrap_file`. It only supports binary reads, and it requires a ``total`` in bytes, because the size of a file-like object can't be determined. The reader does not close the file you pass in, so the caller still owns it::
+
+    from rich.progress import wrap_file
+
+    with open("data.bin", "rb") as raw:
+        with wrap_file(raw, total=1024) as reader:
+            data = reader.read()
+        # raw is still open here
+
